  "slug": "fetal-maternal-d0f2",
  "summary": "Public transcriptomic datasets are routinely combined with machine learning and Mendelian randomization to nominate candidate genes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational candidate-gene prioritization from public transcriptomic resources, spanning neonatal hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, Alzheimer's disease, necrotizing enterocolitis, clear cell renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma, and several exposure- and drug-oriented studies. Common workflows combine differential expression or WGCNA with machine learning feature selection, Mendelian randomization, and network pharmacology or toxicology, often followed by docking or molecular dynamics. Newer elements include post-translational modification framing: the neonatal HIE study integrates 21 PTM gene sets, the AD study builds succinylation-related signatures, and the DMD study addresses isonicotinylation with ensemble learning and in vitro validation. Several studies add clinical cohort or experimental checks, such as NEC clinical cohort validation and the COPD single-cell classifier comparison. Much of the evidence remains computational, so candidates such as CYP1A1, CYP1B1, SLC2A1, and TXNDC17 remain hypotheses pending functional testing.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-19"
}
